## Execution checklist

Work sufficiently: make the smallest requested change, reuse local patterns, and stop when the stated bar is met. Record the task sizing; use the minimal path for a small change without reducing final proof.

Start with the narrowest fast path that can falsify the current increment. Choose it for both what it catches and its invocation-to-answer cost. Run the full gate when the accumulated change makes it meaningful, and always before reporting done. Obtain and honour the project's fast-path and full-gate budgets; when none exist, set and state both.

Reach a falsifiable increment early. Break a large change into proven parts. For a mechanically wide, conceptually narrow change, specify and review one deterministic transform; take available automatic fixes.

Treat recurring gate, fast-path, and setup cost as in-scope: fix it now when it fits this run without displacing the request; otherwise raise it where an owner can act. State any added verification cost or budget breach. Treat inconsistent checks as defects to fix or raise, not evidence to rerun; use failure output that identifies what broke.

Surface ambiguity before building on it. Record a safe assumption and continue when one works; if no assumption can carry the decision, record why before halting. Pass forward the context the next phase would otherwise rediscover.

Explore only the touched area, avoid retrieving material already held, retain only the needed signal, and size exploration to the change within a tracked working-set bound. Spend deliberation on load-bearing decisions. Start independent work together, record it, and keep it moving while a long step runs. Prefer steps that do not need asking; group unavoidable requests. Sync after one proven increment or before a second unverified increment; plan that re-proof.

Debug from a hypothesis. After two failed attempts at one approach or one step that will not take, change tack or submit an honest partial result.

Use the repository's formatter, linter, type checker, tests, and build checks. Add focused behavioral coverage for changed behavior; keep validation, error handling, and security boundaries intact.
